{"timestamp":"2026-07-18T15:28:36.456Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0bd6d73b974b1fab016a5b9ba476c8819187ec1f74a814613e","status":"completed","call_id":"call_uGWpiRiCou5ERIpV4sqEWSyU","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,220p' competitive-eval/core-bench/README.md\",\"workdir\":\"/private/tmp/brain-bench-graph-agentic-pilot\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":18000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"5dfd74e8-4c67-4fb7-a4c3-45f47a5e848d"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-18T15:28:36.646Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_019f75d7-fb26-79c3-aa9b-6705e5bdb221","call_id":"call_uGWpiRiCou5ERIpV4sqEWSyU","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# CORE-Bench Level-2 Feasibility Spike\n\nThis directory contains the preregistered external-retrieval spike described in\n`entire-plan/paper/entire-toolchain/external-corpus-expansion-plan.md`.\n\nThe checked-in files in this directory and `results-core-bench/` are preserved\npre-rename evidence. Their generic runner, analysis, and redaction tools are\nalso preserved because the evidence pins their exact source hashes. Active\nEntire Graph runs use the `core_bench_graph_*` successors and separate output\ndirectories. See [rename provenance](../../ENTIRE-GRAPH-RENAME-PROVENANCE.md).\n\nThe spike uses pinned revisions of:\n\n- `zhangfw123/CORE-Bench` at\n  `682c1e668692a1f28a3351511d88e71fce2809ef`;\n- `ByteDance-Seed/Multi-SWE-bench` at\n  `56ff018c04a38e27ada1e9d0a6d5839a51f88f0d`.\n\nOnly CORE-Bench Level 2's `Multi-SWE-bench` sub-dataset is used for the\nfeasibility spike. A scaled study may add the other Level-2 sources after this\nadapter path passes its go/no-go gates.\n\n## Frozen Selection Protocol\n\n1. Exclude every repository appearing in the frozen fixed-answer artifact,\n   Tasks fixtures, Fidelity manifests, the 240-repository `entire-graph` breadth\n   manifest, or the implementation repositories.\n2. Require an unambiguous mapping from the CORE-Bench repository directory to\n   one upstream Multi-SWE repository and at least three queries.\n3. Rank candidates with SHA-256 using seed `20260711`.\n4. Select one repository per eligible language before filling remaining slots,\n   for eight repositories total.\n5. Select three hash-ranked queries per repository.\n6. Assign two hash-ranked repositories to adapter development and seal six for\n   the smoke run.\n\nThe selection freezer records the complete candidate catalog, every source\nhash, exact base commits, query hashes, candidate-set hashes, and qrel hashes.\nIt never invokes Entire, CBM, file search, BM25, or an LLM.\n\nCORE-Bench can contain duplicate rows for one `(query_id, corpus_id)` pair.\nNative retrieval scoring therefore uses unique corpus IDs while retaining and\nhashing the raw qrel rows. Corpus documents contain text but no path, and the\nsame text can occur in generated and source files. Native chunk scoring accepts\none or more exact tracked locations for a corpus ID; file-level diagnostics use\nonly uniquely located qrels and report that coverage.\n\n## Commands\n\nFrom the `brain-bench` root:\n\n```sh\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_spike.py freeze-exclusions\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_spike.py freeze-manifest\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_spike.py validate-manifest\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_spike.py materialize-development\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_eval.py run \\\n  --role development \\\n  --output competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/development-v1\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_eval.py score \\\n  --role development \\\n  --run-dir competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/development-v1 \\\n  --materialization competitive-eval/core-bench-graph/development-materialization.json \\\n  --output competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/development-v1/scores.json\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_eval.py freeze-protocol \\\n  --development-results competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/development-v1\n```\n\nDownloads are content-pinned and cached under `.cache/`, which is not committed.\nThe generated `overlap-exclusions.json`, `candidate-catalog.json`, and\n`spike-manifest.json` are reviewable benchmark inputs and are committed.\n`development-materialization.json` records exact snapshot trees and qrel\nlocation recovery for the two development repositories. Sealed repositories\ncannot be materialized by this command.\n\nAfter a fresh `core-bench-graph/evaluation-protocol.json` is committed, the\ncurrent-name sealed path is:\n\n```sh\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_spike.py materialize-sealed\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_eval.py run \\\n  --role sealed \\\n  --output competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/sealed-v1\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_eval.py score \\\n  --role sealed \\\n  --run-dir competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/sealed-v1 \\\n  --materialization competitive-eval/core-bench-graph/sealed-materialization.json \\\n  --output competitive-eval/graph-results-core-bench/sealed-v1/scores.json\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/core_bench_graph_analysis.py\n```\n\n## Preserved Historical Redaction Gate\n\nThe preserved runner persisted raw tool diagnostics (`stderr_tail`) in rank and\nscore artifacts. Its byte-identical redaction verifier remains the publication\ngate for the historical evidence:\n\n```sh\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/redact_core_artifacts.py apply\npython3 competitive-eval/tools/redact_core_artifacts.py verify\n```\n\n`apply` removes every persisted `stderr_tail` key, converts screening\n`development_inventory.materializations` entries to repository-relative paths,\nrefreshes each run manifest's `rank_files_sha256` over the redacted rank files\n(preserving the frozen digest under `pre_redaction_rank_files_sha256`), and\nwrites `results-core-bench/redaction-provenance.json` linking every\noriginal/current SHA-256 pair, the exact changed JSON paths, and identical\nbefore/after scoring-projection hashes proving no scoring input changed. The\nfrozen protocol and runner stay byte-identical; the protocol's original\ndevelopment hashes resolve through the provenance manifest. The unchanged\n`feasibility-analysis.json` continues to pin the original score and run-manifest\nbytes through the same bridge. The provenance also freezes\n`core_bench_analysis.py`; running that generator on the redacted scores\nreproduces `feasibility-report.md` byte-identically.\n\n`verify` is idempotent and fails closed on any unknown change, hash or link\nmismatch, remaining `stderr_tail` key, absolute materialization path, or\ngeneric host-path pattern. CI runs it on every push\n(`tests/test_core_redaction.py` plus a direct `verify` step), so an\nunsanitized artifact cannot land.\n\nFresh Entire Graph outputs are not publication-authorized until a separate\nGraph-specific redaction provenance is implemented and verified. The\nhistorical redactor must not be pointed at `graph-results-core-bench/`.\n\n## Evaluation Contract\n\nRank production and scoring are separate processes. The `run` command has no\nqrel or materialization argument and records `qrels_accessed=false` in every\nrank artifact. It reads only the issue, unlabeled candidate corpus, exact\nrepository snapshot, and frozen protocol. The `score` command opens qrels only\nafter ranks have been written.\n\nThe systems are:\n\n- native Okapi BM25 over CORE-Bench candidate chunks;\n- deterministic literal file search over the pinned source tree;\n- `entire-graph symbols`, joined to the same qrel-blind source matches and\n  reranked once by `gpt-5.4`;\n- CBM `search_code`, reranked once by the same model and output schema.\n\nBoth mediated systems receive normalized file, span, symbol, and excerpt\nfields, the same prompt and model, one turn, no filesystem tools, and at most\n60 candidates. A location contributes at most the five candidate corpus chunks\nnearest its focus line, so a broad symbol cannot consume Recall@100 merely by\nspanning a large file. Model failure retains deterministic retrieval order and\nis classified rather than silently retried.\n\n## Canary Result\n\nBefore freezing the sample, the location-recovery contract was checked on the\noverlap-ineligible canary `BurntSushi/ripgrep` PR 1294 at base commit\n`392682d35296bda5c0d0cccf43bae55be3d084df`. All five positive qrel chunks\nmapped by exact text to one tracked location: three in `src/app.rs` and two in\n`src/args.rs`. This canary is excluded by the breadth-corpus overlap rule and\ncannot enter the spike sample.\n\nThe packaged CORE-Bench corpus contains opaque chunk IDs and text, but no file\npaths or base commits. Pinned Multi-SWE filenames identify the upstream\nrepository, and frozen GitHub pull-request metadata supplies its base commit;\nexact-text mapping against tracked files supplies path and line provenance.\nThis avoids downloading source rows that embed hundreds of megabytes of gold\npatches. Missing or ambiguous mappings remain failures and are never resolved\nby consulting the gold patch manually.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"5dfd74e8-4c67-4fb7-a4c3-45f47a5e848d"}}}
