text_result","domain":"huggingface.co","ref_id":"turn7search15","snippet":"<td>python benchmark_decoder_summ.py meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf --aux-model TinyLlama/TinyLlama_v1.1 --dtype fp16</td> ... <td>12324.19</td>","title":"<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Model</th>","url":"https://huggingface.co/datasets/ariG23498/layer-skip-assets/resolve/main/LayerSkip%20HuggingFace%20Benchmarking%20-%20summarization.pdf?download=true"}]}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-18T16:18:11.169Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_019f7605-5e61-7d11-8a76-3b9f731148d6","call_id":"call_7FFZApi6W1w7MiyvLsgF7Hr2","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"README.md · siegelz/core-bench at main (https://huggingface.co/datasets/siegelz/core-bench/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; # Dataset Card for CORE-Bench ... The benchmark is defined in two files: `core_train.json` and `core_test.json` (decrypt the test set using `gpg --output core_test.json --decrypt core_test.json.gpg`).\n\nLanguages: English\n\nArXiv:\n\narxiv: 2409.11363\n\nLicense:\n\nmit\n\n      - en\n    pretty_name: 'CORE: Computational Reproducibility Agent Benchmark'\n    \n\n# Dataset Card for CORE-Bench\n\n`CORE-Bench` is a benchmark evaluating the ability of agents to computationally reproduce scientific papers. It comprises 270 tasks from 90 papers across computer science, social science, and medicine, written in Python or R.\n\nEach task in `CORE-Bench` requires an agent to reproduce the results of a research paper given its repository. The agent must install libraries, packages, and dependencies and run the code. If the code runs successfully, the agent needs to search through all outputs to answer the task questions. The agent submits a report and is evaluated against the results of a successful reproduction. An agent successfully completes a task if it correctly answers all questions about a code repository.\n\n## Dataset Details\n\nThe benchmark is defined in two files: `core_train.json` and `core_test.json` (decrypt the test set using `gpg --output core_test.json --decrypt core_test.json.gpg`).\n\nEach task in the dataset contains the following fields: `field`, `language`, `capsule_title`, `capsule_id`, `task_prompt`, `results`, and `capsule_doi`. The files for each environment are themselves not hosted here. The harness automatically downloads the repositories for each task based on the `capsule_id` from our servers.\n\nNote that the dataset JSON files found in this repository contains the task prompts, task questions, and some other metadata for each task, but not the associated code repositories. The code repositories, which the harness automatically downloads for each task, can be found at https://corebench.cs.princeton.edu/capsules/capsule-XXXXXXX.tar.gz, where XXXXXXX is the `capsule_id`.\n\n## Citation\n\n## Dataset Card Authors\n\nZachary S. Siegel (zss@princeton.edu), Sayash Kapoor (sayashk@princeton.edu), Nitya Nagdir (nn7887@princeton.edu), Benedikt Stroebl (stroebl@princeton.edu), Arvind Narayanan (arvindn@cs.princeton.edu)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - CORE-Bench: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Code Retrieval in the Era of Agentic Coding (https://huggingface.co/papers/2606.11864)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; CORE-Bench addresses the limitations of existing code retrieval benchmarks by introducing a comprehensive evaluation framework for agentic coding tasks that goes beyond simple snippet matching to include repository-level search and context understanding.Generated by Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct ... zhangfw123/CORE-Bench Preview • Updated 5 days ago • 1.62k\nExisting code retrieval benchmarks mainly evaluate docstring-to-function or snippet-level matching, thereby missing this requirement-driven repository search problem. To address this gap, we introduce CORE-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark for code retrieval in the era of agentic coding. CORE-Bench evaluates code retrieval ability at three levels: code understanding, issue-to-edit localization, and broader context retrieval. Built from curated code-search tasks and SWE-bench-series instances, CORE-Bench contains over 180K queries and 106K broader-context relevance labels. Experiments with representative embedding models show a sharp drop from traditional code search to code retrieval in agentic coding settings. Simple supervised fine-tuning of existing embedding models significantly improves performance in this setting, suggesting substantial room for further progress.\n\nView arXiv page View PDF Add to collection\n\n## Models citing this paper 0\n\nNo model linking this paper\n\nCite arxiv.org/abs/2606.11864 in a model README.md to link it from this page.\n\n## Datasets citing this paper 1\n\nzhangfw123/CORE-Bench Preview • Updated 5 days ago • 1.62k\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpdate README.md · siegelz/core-bench at 8e39cf8 (https://huggingface.co/datasets/siegelz/core-bench/commit/8e39cf806b06d1a31f17e90ecd78ce8d96a3339d)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Datasets: ... core-bench ... Dataset card Data Studio Files xet Community\n\n# Datasets:\n\n* * *\n\nsiegelz\n\n/\n\ncore-bench\n\nlike [Button: 9]\n\nLanguages: English\n\nArXiv:\n\narxiv: 2409.11363\n\nLicense:\n\nmit\n\nDataset card Data Studio Files xet Community\n\nImage siegelz commited on Sep 18, 2024\n\nCommit\n\n8e39cf8\n\n·\n\nverified ·\n\n1 Parent(s): 46b4898\n\n# Update README.md\n\nBrowse files\n\nFiles changed (1) hide show\n\n  1. README.md +1 -1\n\nREADME.md CHANGED [Input] Viewed\n\n | @@ -19,7 +19,7 @@ The benchmark is defined in two files: `core_train.json` and `core_test.json` (d\n19  |  \n20  |   Each task in the dataset contains the following fields: `field`, `language`, `capsule_title`, `capsule_id`, `task_prompt`, `results`, and `capsule_doi`. The files for each environment are themselves not hosted here. The harness automatically downloads the repositories for each task based on the `capsule_id` from our servers.\n21  |  \n22  | - Note that the dataset JSON files found in this repository contains the task prompts, task questions, and some other metadata for each task, b