ench/results/result-1781976172.json\nbench/results/result-1781976712.json\nbench/results/result-1781976914.json\nbench/results/result-1781977107.json\nbench/results/result-1781977232.json\nbench/results/result-1781977370.json\nbench/results/result-1781977794.json\nbench/results/result-1781977945.json\nbench/results/result-1781978153.json\nbench/results/result-1781978414.json\nbench/results/result-1781978698.json\nbench/results/result-1781979074.json\nbench/results/result-1781979249.json\nbench/results/result-1781979384.json\nbench/results/result-1781979547.json\nbench/results/result-1781979910.json\nbench/results/result-1781980081.json\nbench/results/result-1781980407.json\nbench/results/result-1781980824.json\nbench/results/result-1781981275.json\nbench/results/result-1781981445.json\nbench/results/result-1781981708.json\nbench/results/result-1781982215.json\nbench/results/result-1781982554.json\nbench/results/result-1781982731.json\nbench/results/result-1781982896.json\nbench/results/result-1781983000.json\nbench/results/result-1781983129.json\nbench/results/result-1781983240.json\nbench/results/result-1781983484.json\nbench/results/result-1781983799.json\nbench/results/result-1781983962.json\nbench/results/result-1781984324.json\nbench/results/result-1781984529.json\nbench/results/result-1781989925.json\nbench/results/result-1781989930.json\nbench/results/result-1781990000.json\nbench/results/result-1781993163.json\nbench/results/result-1781993523.json\nbench/results/result-1781993905.json\nbench/results/result-1781994354.json\nbench/results/result-1781994615.json\nbench/results/result-1781995028.json\nbench/results/result-1781995498.json\nbench/results/result-1781995749.json\nbench/results/result-1781996065.json\nbench/results/result-1781996247.json\nbench/results/result-1781996567.json\nbench/results/result-1781996982.json\nbench/results/result-1781997213.json\nbench/results/result-1781997407.json\nbench/results/result-1781997681.json\nbench/results/result-1781997931.json\nbench/results/result-1781998468.json\nbench/results/result-1781998685.json\nbench/results/result-1781998905.json\nbench/results/result-1781999134.json\nbench/results/result-1781999418.json\nbench/results/result-1782000210.json\nbench/results/result-1782000403.json\nbench/results/result-1782000658.json\nbench/results/result-1782000875.json\nbench/results/result-1782001031.json\nbench/results/result-1782001211.json\nbench/results/result-1782001370.json\nbench/results/result-1782001576.json\nbench/results/result-1782001786.json\nbench/results/result-1782001960.json\nbench/results/result-1782002209.json\nbench/results/result-1782002431.json\ncmd/graph-bench/main.go\ncmd/graph-bench/main_test.go\ndocs/adr/0001-ga-schema-contract.md\ndocs/baseline-report.md\ndocs/benchmarks.md\ndocs/language-support.md\ndocs/operations.md\ndocs/progress-so-far.md\ndocs/semantic_provider_requirements.md\ndocs/v2-plan.md\ndocs/validation/full-plan-local-proof-20260620.md\ninternal/bench/bench.go\ninternal/bench/bench_test.go\ninternal/bench/rss_unix.go\ninternal/bench/rss_windows.go\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"bench/repos.json:2:  \"_comment\": \"Popular GitHub repositories per language for the semantic benchmark: 10 per language for every language the provider supports (see 'entire-graph capabilities'). Entries are owner/name, optionally owner/name@ref to pin a tag/sha in the manifest. Commit pinning for reproducible runs is normally recorded in repos.lock.json via 'graph-bench -update-lock'. Some repos are very large; use -languages/-limit for quick iterations.\",\nREADME.md:38:- [📊 Benchmarks](#-benchmarks)\nREADME.md:45:- [🧪 Benchmark Harness](#-benchmark-harness)\nREADME.md:54:- **🎲 Deterministic. No LLM, no vectors.** Pure tree-sitter static analysis. The same commit always yields the same graph. No embeddings, no similarity thresholds, no fuzzy recall to second-guess. A relation is either in the graph or it is not.\nREADME.md:61:- **💸 Fewer tokens for coding agents.** For a core symbol like `redisCommand` in redis, the five structural questions cost about **2,300 tokens** from the graph versus about **654,000 tokens** to read the 23 files that reference it: a **283x reduction** (measured with tiktoken). Savings scale with how widely a symbol is used.\nREADME.md:62:- **🧩 Part of Entire.** The same graph powers entity-level checkpoint context, so a checkpoint can be judged (keep, revert, continue) without reading the full diff.\nREADME.md:133:  ~ function validate_token signature changed (14 dependents)\nREADME.md:135:  - function parse_token removed (0 dependents)\nREADME.md:160:provider version, and reports `index_cache_hit` plus indexing latency. It also\nREADME.md:165:latency-sensitive work.\nREADME.md:188:- **Bounded output.** Results are budgeted to **16 KiB** of serialized snippets by default, sized to drop straight into a model context window. Tune with `--top-k` and `--max-context-bytes` (`--max-context-bytes 0` for an unbounded diagnostic ranking).\nREADME.md:189:- **Agent-native output.** `--format agent` starts with auditable cache, index, preselection, query, and total latency telemetry, then emits only ranked locations, symbol names, and focused source. When the full header would crowd out a ranked location, tight positive budgets use compact telemetry when it fits; the complete wire output never exceeds the requested bytes. Use `json` or `ndjson` when a program needs the full result schema, completeness, partial failures, and search diagnostics.\nREADME.md:190:- **Focused regions.** Semantic results span at most 80 lines by default, keeping locations precise while snippets remain independently capped at 40 lines. Override with `--max-region-lines` and `--max-snippet-lines` when broader context is useful.\nREADME.md:191:- **Cold search** scans files without parsing, then indexes an adaptive pool of 96–256 query-relevant files based on the requested `--top-k`. Shallow searches of up to 20 results retain the 96-file interactive latency bound. Deeper rankings add a corpus-wide sparse pass over eligible files up to 2 MiB, while syntax parsing remains bounded to the adaptive pool; this deliberately spends more time for region recall inside large files. Override the parsing pool with `--max-indexed-files`, or force exhaustive parsing with `--index-all-files`.\nREADME.md:266:Another **149 filetypes** are recognized as inventory-only, and everything else is reported as an honest partial failure rather than dropped silently. See [docs/language-support.md](docs/language-support.md) for the full two-tier matrix. Inventory-only coverage is never counted as semantic parity in benchmarks.\nREADME.md:278:  Output is bounded to 16 KiB by default, sized for direct inclusion in a model context window.\nREADME.md:332:Answering the five structural questions for a core symbol, graph query vs reading every referencing file (tiktoken `cl100k`):\nREADME.md:336:| `redisCommand` (redis), 23 referencing files | ~2,300 tokens | ~654,000 tokens | **283x** |\nREADME.md:340:Every number here is reproducible with the benchmark harness (see [Benchmark Harness](#-benchmark-harness) and [docs/benchmarks.md](docs/benchmarks.md)).\nREADME.md:350:- **Explicit preindex:** `index --head` builds and verifies that same query-independent artifact before latency-sensitive agent work; cached `search` and `neighbors` calls then report the hit directly.\nREADME.md:377:- `search --query ...` emits ranked, qrel-blind source regions as JSON, NDJSON, text, or compact agent output, bounded to 16 KiB by default; machine formats include partial failures, completeness, and phase-separated latency telemetry.\nREADME.md:411:| `--top-k` / `--max-context-bytes` | Search result count and byte budget |\nREADME.md:422:  graph-bench/      Reproducible benchmark driver\nREADME.md:426:docs/               Language support matrix, benchmarks, operations, provider requirements\nREADME.md:436:Every performance and quality number is reproducible with `cmd/graph-bench`:\nREADME.md:440:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -update-lock\nREADME.md:443:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -manifest bench/repos.fast.json\nREADME.md:446:go run ./cmd/graph-bench\nREADME.md:449:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -languages Go,Rust -limit 3\nREADME.md:450:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -profile syntax-only -languages Go -limit 1\nREADME.md:453:Read speed and throughput from the `fast` profile (and `syntax-only` for the floor), and semantic depth and coverage from `full`. See [docs/benchmarks.md](docs/benchmarks.md) for the full harness layout, flags, and guardrails.\nbench/README.md:3:`graph-bench` clones popular real-world repositories per language and measures the\nbench/README.md:12:  `NoNetwork` set, so the path we benchmark is the same local-only path the\nbench/README.md:42:| `cmd/graph-bench`          | CLI driver (clone + measure + report).                 |\nbench/README.md:48:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -manifest bench/repos.fast.json\nbench/README.md:51:go run ./cmd/graph-bench\nbench/README.md:54:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -update-lock\nbench/README.md:57:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -languages Go,Rust -limit 3\nbench/README.md:60:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -skip-clone\nbench/README.md:63:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -out -\nbench/repos.fast.json:2:  \"_comment\": \"Fast tier: a curated subset of repos.json (3 small/medium repos per language, no mega-repos) for quick per-phase runs. Every entry is also in repos.json, so it is pinned by the shared repos.lock.json and reuses the same clone cache. Run: graph-bench -manifest bench/repos.fast.json\",\ndocs/benchmarks.md:4:WP10. The harness lives in `cmd/graph-bench` (driver) and `internal/bench`\ndocs/benchmarks.md:11:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -update-lock\ndocs/benchmarks.md:14:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -manifest bench/repos.fast.json\ndocs/benchmarks.md:17:go run ./cmd/graph-bench\ndocs/benchmarks.md:20:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -languages Go,Rust -limit 3\ndocs/benchmarks.md:21:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -skip-clone\ndocs/benchmarks.md:29:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -profile syntax-only -languages Go -limit 1 \\\ndocs/benchmarks.md:40:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -profile syntax-only -languages Go -limit 3\ndocs/benchmarks.md:43:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -profile fast -languages Go,Python -limit 5\ndocs/benchmarks.md:46:go run ./cmd/graph-bench -profile full -languages Go,Python,TypeScript -limit 5\ndocs/benchmarks.md:104:before the benchmark measured `StreamSnapshot` and before profiles existed**.\ndocs/benchmarks.md:105:Treat the numbers as historical; re-run with the current streaming benchmark\ndocs/benchmarks.md:110:  context on the literal's line cut that to 206 (real registrations only).\ndocs/benchmarks.md:127:  tensorflow. The streaming path (`StreamSnapshot`, now the benchmark's measured\ndocs/baseline-report.md:8:machine-readable golden baselines under\ndocs/baseline-report.md:9:`internal/sem/testdata/fixtures/*.ndjson.golden`, which are the authoritative\ndocs/baseline-report.md:14:`internal/sem/golden_test.go` (`TestProviderGoldenSnapshots`) snapshots each\ndocs/baseline-report.md:16:compares the full NDJSON stream against `<name>.ndjson.golden`. Any change to\ndocs/baseline-report.md:17:symbols, relations, externals, or header stats appears as a golden diff in\ndocs/baseline-report.md:23:goldens; it is not a substitute for broader external scored corpora.\ndocs/baseline-report.md:37:  its name in `goldenFixtures`, run with `-update`, then update\ndocs/baseline-report.md:148:intentionally captured in the goldens so improvements show up as diffs.\ndocs/baseline-report.md:154:  now recorded only when its line carries routing context (an HTTP-verb/route\ndocs/baseline-report.md:191:Fixed (kept as a note so the goldens explain the change):\ndocs/baseline-report.md:295:- **Rust calls are not captured (`rust-basic`).** `token.validate()`, the\ndocs/baseline-report.md:318:- Header `benchmark_profile`: emitted only by benchmark tooling (WP10); omitted\ndocs/validation/full-plan